# Phase 5.4 — CPU Evolution Strategies and Augmented Random Search

This release builds on Phase 5.3 without changing the Tron rules, DQN, PPO,
scripted opponents, observation encoder, common benchmark or Pygame interface.
It adds an **optimizer-by-representation CPU comparison** to test whether the
legacy genetic algorithm is the limiting factor in Neuroevolution.

## Recommended four-way study

| Variant | Policy representation | Optimisation method |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `cpu_ga_legacy` | Legacy 192,467-parameter dense network | Original elitist genetic algorithm |
| `cpu_es_legacy` | Same legacy dense network | Antithetic Evolution Strategies (ES) |
| `cpu_ars_linear` | Linear full-board 35,103-parameter network | Augmented Random Search (ARS) |
| `cpu_es_linear` | Same linear full-board network | Antithetic ES |

All methods receive the same 13-channel full-board observation, choose among
Left / Straight / Right, and learn from the **unchanged terminal reward**:
win +1, draw 0, loss -1. The linear representation is exactly a linear mapping
from the full-board observation to three action scores, with no new privileged
features. Antithetic ES and ARS evaluate `θ + σε` and `θ − σε` on **identical**
paired starting configurations, opponent choices and random seeds. Opponent
quotas are balanced within each iteration. ES uses mirrored finite differences;
ARS uses top-ranked mirrored directions with fitness normalisation. Global L2
update clipping is a numerical safeguard. No backpropagation is used.

These are different architectures *and* search methods. Differences between
`cpu_ga_legacy` vs `cpu_es_legacy`, and `cpu_ars_linear` vs `cpu_es_linear`,
isolate the optimiser. Cross-architecture differences do not isolate it.
The key comparison axis is **active training seconds**, not number of games.

## Installation — Ubuntu on WSL

Download the ZIP to the Windows Downloads folder. In Ubuntu:

```bash
cd ~/ai_games
unzip "/mnt/c/Users/Local Admin/Downloads/tron_light_cycles_phase5_4.zip"
cd tron_light_cycles_phase5_4
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
python -m pip install -e '.[dqn,ppo,play,dev,analysis]'
pytest -q
```

If your Windows profile folder is not literally `Local Admin`, replace that
part of the path with the actual profile-directory name.

## First a one-minute-per-variant pilot

```bash
python -m tron_ai.evolution_study \
    --seconds 60 \
    --eval-interval 30 \
    --eval-pairs 4 \
    --seeds 2026 \
    --variants cpu_ga_legacy cpu_es_legacy cpu_ars_linear cpu_es_linear \
    --out results/cpu_search_pilot
```

This is a software and throughput pilot, **not** a reliable algorithm ranking.
The total requested active training budget is four minutes, plus evaluation,
checkpointing and generation-boundary overshoot.

## Five-minute-per-variant comparison

```bash
python -m tron_ai.evolution_study \
    --seconds 300 \
    --eval-interval 60 \
    --eval-pairs 8 \
    --seeds 2026 \
    --variants cpu_ga_legacy cpu_es_legacy cpu_ars_linear cpu_es_linear \
    --out results/cpu_search_5min
```

This is **20 minutes of requested active training in total**, plus evaluation
overhead and generation-boundary overshoot. It launches one subprocess at a
time and caps BLAS/OpenMP threads to one for comparable CPU budgets. Each run
writes `best.npz`, `evaluations.jsonl`, `episodes.jsonl`,
`generation_stats.jsonl`, timing metadata and checkpoint snapshots. The study
writes `comparison.csv`, `aggregate_by_variant.csv`, `comparison.png`, and a
manifest recording every exact command. Use a fresh output directory per run.

To generate additional plots for one run:

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train plot results/cpu_search_5min/cpu_ars_linear_seed2026
explorer.exe results/cpu_search_5min
```

For ES and ARS, `search_diagnostics.png` reports mirrored trial fitness,
parameter-update size, how often positive and negative perturbations differ,
and the mean rewards by opponent. Existing plot outputs are retained.

To play the **best** saved evolutionary checkpoint (any of GA, ES or ARS):

```bash
python -m tron_ai.play --checkpoint results/cpu_search_5min/cpu_ars_linear_seed2026/best.npz
```

All variants save `algorithm=evolution` and their architecture / optimiser in
checkpoint metadata, so the established Pygame player and reference evaluator
can load them without an additional interface.

## Further controlled evaluation

After the pilot, repeat promising configurations with multiple seeds; for
example:

```bash
python -m tron_ai.evolution_study \
    --seconds 300 --eval-interval 60 --eval-pairs 8 \
    --seeds 2027 2028 \
    --variants cpu_ga_legacy cpu_es_legacy cpu_ars_linear cpu_es_linear \
    --out results/cpu_search_more_seeds
```

**Keep the independent holdout seeds untouched until final selection.** The
64-game fixed validation suite with `--eval-pairs 8` is sufficient for learning
curves but too small for definitive rankings, especially when selecting the
best of multiple checkpoints. Do not infer superiority from smoke-test scores.

## Direct command-line examples

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train evolution \
    --optimizer ars --architecture linear --directions 12 --top-directions 6 \
    --fitness-pairs 8 --noise-std 0.025 --step-size 0.01 \
    --seconds 300 --eval-interval 60 --eval-pairs 8 \
    --out results/ars_linear_direct

python -m tron_ai.train evolution \
    --optimizer es --architecture legacy --directions 12 \
    --fitness-pairs 8 --noise-std 0.025 --step-size 0.01 \
    --seconds 300 --eval-interval 60 --eval-pairs 8 \
    --out results/es_legacy_direct
```

Notes: ES/ARS batch sizes are defined by mirrored directions and fitness pairs;
`--population` and `--elites` belong to the genetic algorithm only. ES/ARS
checkpoints can be warm-started with `--init-from` when the architecture matches;
this reloads policy weights but does **not** resume the optimiser state. The
previous project and algorithms are still present.

