# Tron Light Cycles — Phase 5: controlled experiments

Phase 5 adds serial comparison and continuous curricula without resetting the learner or historical pool. All previous algorithms, results and gameplay remain available. See the historical documentation below for individual algorithm options.

## Install (Ubuntu / WSL)

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
python -m pip install -e '.[dqn,ppo,play,dev,analysis]'
pytest -q
```

## Recommended first experiment: survival baseline

```bash
python -m tron_ai.experiment --seconds 300 --eval-interval 60 --eval-pairs 8 --seeds 2026 --profile default --out results/baseline_5min
```

One subprocess at a time, 300 active seconds per learner (15 minutes total active training, plus evaluation, setup and logging). Each evaluation uses 64 games across the four scripted references. Use `--seeds 2026 2027 2028` for three independent runs per algorithm; order rotates by seed. Use `--profile pilot` in a separate output directory to compare alternative *candidate* settings; these have not been tuned. `--profile smoke` is only for rapid pipeline checks. Keep validation schedules identical across configurations. Do not choose hyperparameters using holdout scores.

## Continuous curriculum

```bash
python -m tron_ai.experiment --seconds 1800 --eval-interval 180 --eval-pairs 8 --seeds 2026 --curriculum survival:0.4,space:0.25,competitive:0.15,selfplay:0.2 --out results/curriculum_30min
```

Each fraction is a share of requested active time, not episode count. Fractions must be positive and sum to one. Stages switch at the next episode or generation boundary, with an extra checkpoint at a crossed boundary. Very short stages may be skipped if a batch overshoots. `stages.json` and episode logs record the actual schedule. The learner, optimizer (if applicable), exploration state, population and historical pool stay in memory across transitions. The self-play stage uses 40% same-algorithm historical policies when available. There is no cross-algorithm training pool or mastery-based progression yet.

## Reporting and held-out evaluation

The top-level output directory contains `manifest.json`, `comparison.csv`, `aggregate.json`, `comparison.png`, child logs, and one directory per algorithm/seed. Each run preserves checkpoint metadata, raw evaluation and training rows, stage transitions and time breakdown. `--report-only --out PATH` rebuilds the report for fully completed runs. The manifest stores exact process commands (including machine-specific absolute paths), not a portable replay script. Use the CLI to reproduce a run on another machine. For an individual curve: `python -m tron_ai.train plot PATH/dqn_seed2026`.

After choosing a single final protocol using validation only, run a fresh multi-seed experiment with `--holdout-pairs 20 --holdout-seed 271828183` (160 held-out reference games per selected policy). Holdout is off by default. The new seed is reserved for final evaluation; do not tune on it. Selection remains based on highest validation score (wins + half draws), with earliest checkpoint winning ties. A zero-time checkpoint may be selected. This seed is independent of the earlier smoke holdout. Scores and strict win rates are different. Plot actual training times rather than assuming budgets are exact. CPU thread limits are requested, not hardware isolation guarantees. Close other demanding programs when benchmarking. For formal comparisons use multiple seeds and report variability; timings are hardware-dependent. No exact resume from interrupted training; use a new output directory or report completed runs only.

## Bundled Phase 5 smoke run

`results/phase5_smoke/` contains a four-second requested-budget curriculum test on each algorithm, eight validation games per checkpoint, one seed, no holdout. The evolutionary run was restarted after the execution wrapper interrupted it. Its actual 6.35 seconds substantially exceeds the target due to generation boundaries. All three reached the self-play stage, but only PPO actually sampled historical opponents during this tiny run. These results are plumbing checks, not a fair ranking or trained presentation models.

## Historical documentation

# Tron Light Cycles — Phase 4B: PPO

This standalone release adds CPU PyTorch Proximal Policy Optimisation (PPO) alongside Double DQN. The engine, observation encoder, rewards, scripted agents, tournament and common timing/evaluation runner are unchanged. All previous results are preserved.

## Quick start (Ubuntu / WSL)

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
python -m pip install -e '.[dqn,ppo,play,dev,analysis]'
pytest -q
python -m tron_ai.play --checkpoint results/dqn_smoke/best.npz --checkpoint results/ppo_smoke/checkpoints/checkpoint_0003.npz --opponent ppo
```

Keys 1–4 select scripted opponents; 5 selects DQN and 6 selects PPO when both are loaded. If only PPO is loaded it is key 5. Repeat `--checkpoint` once per algorithm; models must have identical board configurations. The PPO final checkpoint contains updated weights but is not stronger on the recorded fixed evaluation. The PPO `best.npz` is the untrained baseline because all four validation scores tied and the common selection rule keeps the earliest snapshot. Neither bundled learner is a calibrated presentation opponent. To check graphics locally with both models use the same play command with `--smoke-test` (optionally `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy` for a headless check).

## PPO training

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train ppo --seconds 300 --eval-interval 60 --eval-pairs 4 --stage survival --out results/ppo_survival
python -m tron_ai.train plot results/ppo_survival
python -m tron_ai.train evaluate results/ppo_survival/best.npz --pairs 20 --split holdout --out results/ppo_survival_holdout
```

Use a fresh output directory each time. After survival improves, warm-start a new curriculum stage with `--init-from results/ppo_survival/best.npz --stage space` and a different `--out`. This resets the optimizer, rollout, random stream and historical pool, not exact training resumption. `--stage selfplay` enables the existing historical pool. No automatic curriculum, GPU training or full optimizer-state resumption is provided.

The actor–critic shares the DQN global CNN and local head-centred 5 × 5 feature encoder, with distinct policy logits and scalar state-value heads. The initial actor is approximately uniform. Training samples categorical actions (no epsilon-greedy); evaluation and gameplay also sample the learned policy using independent seeded RNGs, rather than forcing its argmax. No additional state, action masking or reward shaping. The critic is trained using GAE returns, with terminal masks across game boundaries and value bootstrap at a nonterminal rollout boundary. Rollouts may span matches. Old action log probabilities are frozen during the clipped policy updates. Each completed rollout is discarded after its update epochs. A partially filled final rollout is not optimized; the pending step count is logged. Evaluations do not clear the rollout or change learner weights. This preserves the common episode-boundary time-budget rules and avoids extra unbudgeted updates.

Defaults: 512 rollout steps, minibatch 64, 4 epochs, learning rate 0.0003, gamma 0.99, GAE lambda 0.95, clip 0.2, entropy coefficient 0.02, value coefficient 0.5, max gradient norm 0.5, one CPU thread. See `python -m tron_ai.train ppo --help`. Rollout size must be divisible by minibatch size.

`evaluations.jsonl` logs collection policy entropy (maximum ln(3) ≈ 1.099 nats), training action fractions, policy/value losses, update entropy, approximate KL, clip fraction, optimizer steps, completed rollouts and pending steps. Metrics summarize each preceding evaluation interval except cumulative counts. Null means no applicable samples. `train plot` also produces `ppo_diagnostics.png`. Watch for low entropy and domination by one action, but use win rates and gameplay to assess strategy. High entropy alone does not imply effective learning.

### Bundled PPO smoke experiment

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train ppo --seconds 12 --eval-interval 4 --eval-pairs 2 --rollout-steps 128 --batch-size 32 --epochs 4 --out results/ppo_smoke_repeat
```

Measured run: 12.0017 active seconds, 3,171 environment steps, 356 episodes, 24 completed rollouts, 384 optimizer updates, 99 pending steps. Validation stayed at 4 wins / 0 draws / 12 losses at all four snapshots (all wins against Random). Baseline and final snapshots each scored 2 wins / 1 draw / 29 losses in the 32-game holdout. No measured improvement; this is a plumbing check, not a comparison of tuned algorithms. Collection entropy remained approximately 1.090–1.098 nats. Training actions at the last interval were 32.4% left, 37.9% straight, 29.7% right. No observed right-turn collapse in this short run. Longer runs and multiple seeds are necessary. The two holdouts are documented for diagnostics, not used to select a checkpoint.

---

## Phase 4A: Double DQN (previous release)

This version adds a CPU PyTorch Double DQN learner and playable DQN checkpoints to Phase 3. Prior gameplay, tournament and training infrastructure remain available.

## Phase 4A quick start (Ubuntu / WSL)

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
# CPU PyTorch wheel; avoid installing unnecessary CUDA packages.
python -m pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
python -m pip install -e '.[dqn,play,dev,analysis]'
pytest -q
python -m tron_ai.play --checkpoint results/dqn_smoke/best.npz
```

Press 5 to select DQN (default when a checkpoint is supplied); keys 1–4 still select scripted opponents. Checkpoint board settings are used automatically. The bundled model is a weak early smoke-test agent, not a validated presentation opponent. To train a longer run:

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train dqn --seconds 300 --eval-interval 60 --eval-pairs 4 --stage survival --epsilon-decay 20000 --out results/dqn_survival
python -m tron_ai.train plot results/dqn_survival
python -m tron_ai.train evaluate results/dqn_survival/best.npz --pairs 20 --split holdout --out results/dqn_survival_holdout
python -m tron_ai.play --checkpoint results/dqn_survival/best.npz
```

Use new output directories for each run. Evaluation games are additional to active training time. CPU threads default to 1; use the same thread count for controlled comparisons. `--epsilon-decay` is measured in environment steps, not seconds. The learner uses the original full 13-channel board, with a global CNN and a learned head-centred 5 × 5 branch, uniform bit-packed replay, Double DQN targets, Huber loss, Adam, and gradient norm clipping at 10. No action masking, reward shaping, or opponent-specific features are supplied. Terminal outcomes do not bootstrap. The 16,384-transition default replay allocates about 46 MiB for packed observation arrays, excluding batch tensors and metadata. There is no GPU support in this release.

Hyperparameters are configurable (`python -m tron_ai.train dqn --help`). `evaluations.jsonl` contains epsilon, replay size, optimizer update count, and mean TD loss since the preceding evaluation (null before any update). The fixed benchmark still records action distributions and win rates. Best checkpoints maximize wins + half draws. Each checkpoint contains inference weights, not a complete training state. `--init-from PATH` initializes a new run from a DQN checkpoint but resets optimizer, exploration, replay, time and historical opponent pool. Full exact training resumption and automated curriculum advancement remain unimplemented.

Measured bundled smoke command (12 seconds, not a tuned experiment):

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train dqn --seconds 12 --eval-interval 4 --eval-pairs 2 --warmup 128 --batch-size 32 --epsilon-decay 2000 --target-every 50 --out results/new_smoke
```

The best snapshot won 6/16 validation games (37.5%); independent holdout 14/32 (43.75%): Random 8/8, Safe 6/8, Space 0/8, Territory 0/8. These small single-seed samples are not proof of robust learning or relative algorithm superiority. The included `results/dqn_smoke` and `results/dqn_holdout` contain raw outcomes and weights. Python/PyTorch version and settings are recorded in run config.

## 1. Setup (Ubuntu/WSL)

Extract the new ZIP alongside Phase 1, not over it. From the new directory:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install -e '.[play,dev]'
pytest -q
python -m tron_ai.play
```

On WSL2 with WSLg, the window should open on the Windows desktop. If display initialization fails, check `echo "$DISPLAY"` in Ubuntu and run `wsl --update` from Windows PowerShell; older WSL setups may require a configured X server. Pygame is optional for headless engine use (`.[dev]` only).

### Play

- Cyan is the human cycle; orange is the AI opponent.
- Space starts a round with a three-second countdown, pauses/resumes during play, or starts the next round after a result.
- Arrow keys or WASD select an absolute heading. U-turns are ignored. Only the first valid turn per movement tick is buffered.
- 1–4 chooses an opponent before or between matches. Scores are tracked separately for each opponent during the session.
- + / - changes speed (3–16 moves/sec; default 8); F11 toggles fullscreen; Esc exits.
- Opening sides alternate after each completed round. The human interface deliberately uses fixed horizontal openings; randomized mirrored openings remain available for later training.

| Key | Opponent | Logic |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Random actions | Uniformly samples all three relative moves |
| 2 | Random safe | Samples immediately wall/trail-safe moves |
| 3 | Flood-fill survival (default) | Maximises accessible cells and available exits |
| 4 | Territory control | One-turn worst-case opponent lookahead, evaluates reachable territory using shortest-path distances |

These are heuristic reference policies, ranked by the scripted tournament below, not calibrated human difficulty levels. They receive the same frozen game state as each other, never the human's pending input. Safe actions cannot guarantee survival because moves are simultaneous. The neural agents later will all receive the identical observation encoder described below.

```bash
python -m tron_ai.play --opponent territory --speed 6 --fullscreen
SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy python -m tron_ai.play --smoke-test
```

The smoke test exercises ready, countdown, playing, paused and finished screens. The terminal demo is still available (`python -m tron_ai --games 5 --show-board`).

### New modules

- `opponents.py`: shared scripted policy interface, legal-move and flood-fill utilities, four reference policies.
- `session.py`: human input buffering, round state machine, pause/countdown, speed, scorekeeping, atomic simulation stepping.
- `play.py`: resizable, fullscreen-capable Pygame frontend with crash indicators and scoreboard.
- `tests/test_phase2.py`: controller, opponent and optional graphical smoke tests.

Validation caveat: Pygame was unavailable in the build environment, so graphical execution and WSLg integration must be verified locally. See VALIDATION.md.

## 2. Small API example

```python
from tron_ai import Action, TronEnv

env = TronEnv(seed=42)                         # board 30 x 24
state = env.reset(seed=123, mode="random_mirrored")
while not state.done:
    observation_0 = env.observe(0)              # (13, 30, 30), uint8
    observation_1 = env.observe(1)              # both from SAME state
    # Replace these placeholder actions with independent policies.
    action_0, action_1 = Action.STRAIGHT, Action.STRAIGHT
    result = env.step((action_0, action_1))    # only ONE atomic step
    state = result.state
    print(result.rewards, result.collisions)
print("winner", state.winner, "reason", state.end_reason)
```

A real training loop should choose both actions *from their pre-step observations*, then call `step((a0, a1))` exactly once. This avoids any algorithm receiving a first-move advantage. `advance(state, (a0, a1), config)` is a pure functional alternative that returns a `StepResult` without changing the input state. `TronEnv.load_state(state)` validates an externally supplied state for controlled tests/replays.

## 3. Game rules (fixed contract)

| Field | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| Default board | 30 columns × 24 rows, origin top left, increasing y downward |
| Players | Two simultaneously moving cycles |
| Actions | **0 = left, 1 = straight, 2 = right**, relative to current direction |
| Occupancy | Every previously visited cell, **including both current head positions**, blocks all future entry |
| Moves | Each policy acts using the same frozen previous state; turns and destinations are computed before collision decisions |
| Wall collision | Moving outside `[0, width) × [0, height)` crashes |
| Trail collision | Entering either player's visited cell crashes |
| Free-cell head-on | Both targeting the same free cell gives a draw |
| Head-swap | Both attempting to exchange occupied head cells gives a draw |
| One crash | Survivor wins, even if the survivor enters its own next free cell in that terminal turn |
| Two crashes | Draw; this includes both hitting walls, trails, or both targeting an occupied cell |
| Step limit | Draw if both survive the final allowed turn |
| Rewards | On win: `(1,-1)` or `(-1,1)`; on draw or nonterminal move: `(0,0)` |
| Termination | `result.done`, `result.winner`, `result.end_reason`; can't step a finished game |

Failed attempts do **not** add out-of-bounds or colliding target cells to the trails. A `StepResult` retains both `attempted_heads` and two per-player `collisions` for later Pygame impact effects.

### Starting positions

`reset(mode="fixed")` places players at mirrored horizontal positions on the centre row, facing inward. `reset(mode="fixed", swap_sides=True)` reverses player assignments. `reset(mode="random_mirrored", seed=123)` randomly varies the mirrored start distance, starting row/column, player-side assignment **and whether the players face horizontally or vertically inward**. A given seed fully reproduces the opening. You can force an orientation using `axis="horizontal"` or `axis="vertical"` for either mode. For evaluation, publish/freeze the seed list and balance both sides and orientations (using explicit `swap_sides` and `axis` or paired games).

## 4. Shared 13-channel observation

**Every AI receives exactly the same information** through `env.observe(player)` and the same `Action` and reward semantics. Neural-network architectures may differ. Observations are `np.uint8`, contiguous, channel-first `(13, S, S)` where `S=max(width,height)` (default `(13,30,30)`). Since the board is rectangular, it is centred and padded into a square for a **fixed-shaped observation** regardless of rotation. Padded cells are flagged as out-of-bounds in channel 12. The whole image is rotated so **the observing player's heading points north**, and own/opponent identities are relabelled accordingly. This is player-relative, not world-relative.

| Channel | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | Own visited cells (includes own head) |
| 1 | Opponent visited cells (includes opponent head) |
| 2 | Own current head (one-hot location) |
| 3 | Opponent current head (one-hot location) |
| 4–7 | Own heading N/E/S/W (one-hot at own head; only N active after normalisation) |
| 8–11 | Opponent heading N/E/S/W in observer-relative coordinates (one-hot at opponent head) |
| 12 | Padding outside the actual grid (physical walls); square border is an implicit wall |

Both agents' head/trail positions, both headings, and the playable region are recoverable. No target action, opponent future intent, rewards or privileged information is leaked. Keep this encoding fixed across the three algorithms, including benchmark evaluations. When using a PyTorch network, convert `uint8` batches to `float32` before passing through the model.

**Memory note:** these dense observations require `13×30×30 = 11,700` bytes each (uncompressed). Replay buffers containing many transitions will need deliberate memory planning in the DQN phase. The engine itself stores the underlying positions compactly as frozensets.

## 5. Design and test boundaries

- `src/tron_ai/engine.py`: rules, state dataclasses, pure transition function, reset, state validation.
- `src/tron_ai/observations.py`: shared observation encoding, no rendering or training dependencies.
- `src/tron_ai/__main__.py`: small terminal smoke demo with two random-action agents, **not** trained policies.
- `tests/test_engine.py`: exact collision, terminal reward, reset, action and validation cases.
- `tests/test_observations.py`: shape, channel semantics, player-relative alignment, direction remapping, padding, buffer safety.
- `tests/test_properties.py`: 500 seeded random full games checking invariants and 1,000 state/turn comparisons with swapped player labels.

Phase 3 now adds the shared training interface, fixed reference evaluation suite, opponent pool, logging and checkpoint infrastructure (see the guide below).

## Scripted-agent tournament (new)

The tournament uses the original Phase 2 engine and policies without changes.
Install the optional plotting dependency:

```bash
python -m pip install -e '.[play,dev,analysis]'
python -m tron_ai.tournament --pairs 100 --seed 2026 --workers 1 --out results/my_run
```

For a quicker smoke run use `--pairs 2 --no-plots --out results/smoke`.
Use `--workers 4` to run games in parallel, if you have spare cores. The
reference run in `results/tournament` used four workers; choose a different
output directory to preserve the reference results. The runner overwrites files
with matching names in its output directory.

Each of the six distinct agent pairings uses the same sampled openings and
agent RNG seeds. Each opening is played twice with sides exchanged; axes
alternate between horizontal and vertical. The standard run has 1,200 games,
200 per matchup and 600 per agent. The diagonal (self-matches) is not played.
Policies choose from the same immutable pre-turn game state. No rendering,
learning, reward shaping or policy modifications are used.

Outputs:

- `matches.csv`: one row per game, with seeds, starting coordinates, result,
  duration in turns, termination reason, and policy CPU times.
- `matchups.csv`: wins, losses, draws and average turns for each pairing.
- `ranking.csv`: aggregate win/draw/loss rates, mean reward, tournament score
  (win=1, draw=0.5, loss=0), and CPU milliseconds per decision (total policy CPU
  time divided by total moves, including terminal moves).
- `metadata.json`: run configuration, host information and elapsed time.
- `win_rate_heatmap.png`, `outcomes.png`: plots (unless `--no-plots`).

CPU timing excludes the engine, observation encoding, rendering and training;
it measures process CPU time, not wall-clock latency. Parallel execution and
hardware can affect these numbers. Run with a single worker on your machine for
an isolated local timing comparison. Games sharing the same opening are paired,
not independent samples; these results are descriptive rather than a formal
statistical test. Scripted opponents read raw game state, rather than the neural
observation tensor. The result establishes reference difficulty against this
pool, not human difficulty or universal dominance. No training has occurred.

## Phase 3 — Shared training guide

Install with `python -m pip install -e '.[play,dev,analysis]'` (omit `play` for headless-only use). Run from the project directory with the virtualenv activated:

```bash
pytest -q
python -m tron_ai.train reference --seconds 30 --eval-interval 10 --eval-pairs 4 --stage survival --out results/reference_run
python -m tron_ai.train plot results/reference_run
python -m tron_ai.train evaluate results/reference_run/best.npz --pairs 10 --split holdout --out results/reference_holdout
explorer.exe results/reference_run
```

For a fast smoke test, use `--seconds 2 --eval-interval 1 --eval-pairs 1` and a fresh `--out` directory. The runner refuses to overwrite nonempty output directories. All evaluation games use the same board configuration as the saved model. `--pairs N` means N paired openings per opponent, each played twice, so an evaluation contains 8N games. Use even N for equal horizontal/vertical proportions. The standalone evaluate command can also repeat validation using `--split validation` and the original `--pairs` value. Longer and multiple-seed experiments will be needed for meaningful performance claims. Keep the holdout separate from model selection and tuning.

### Architecture

- `training/core.py`: observation-only Actor/Learner protocols, a transition (observation, action, reward, next observation, done), episode runner and adapter to the existing `Policy` interface.
- `training/benchmark.py`: deterministic paired validation and separate holdout seeds; fresh frozen actors and opponents per match; aggregate and opponent-specific outcome metrics.
- `training/pool.py`: weighted scripted pools and frozen historical actors, at most 12 by default; preserves best and recent snapshots. On selfplay, 40% historical, selected equally among best/latest/uniform modes (which can select the same checkpoint). If history is empty, use scripted only.
- `training/checkpoints.py`: atomic compressed NumPy policy snapshots; numeric arrays and JSON metadata, without pickle. These are inference snapshots, **not** exact-resume training states. Full training-state persistence is deferred; DQN supports weights-only warm starts.
- `training/runner.py`: serial time-budgeted loop, baseline + periodic + final evaluations, best score selection (ties retain earliest), logging. No Pygame rendering.
- `training/reference.py`: a tiny stochastic linear policy with a terminal reward-weighted, episode-averaged policy-gradient update. It receives the common full board then internally extracts immediate free-space features. Its role is to verify learning plumbing; it is not DQN, PPO, Neuroevolution or a competitive fourth ML agent.
- `training/plots.py`: offline diagnostic plots, active time and environment steps, rates per opponent, action fractions.

A learner implements `act(observation)`, `update(transition)`, `snapshot()` (numeric arrays), and an algorithm name. An actor factory `(weights, board_config, seed) -> Actor` must instantiate a fresh inference-only actor without mutating the training learner. Future algorithms can accumulate replay, rollouts or population fitness inside the learner; algorithm-specific batch scheduling, final rollout handling, optimizer/population persistence and entropy diagnostics are Phase 4 work. For population search, every fitness game must be counted and timed; do not report only champion games. Policy snapshots can be wrapped with `ObservationPolicy` to use the existing session interface once algorithm-specific loaders are added. The Pygame menu also selects a DQN checkpoint when supplied with --checkpoint.

### Manual curriculum (opponent probabilities per episode)

| Stage flag | Random | Safe | Space | Territory | History |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| survival | 50% | 50% | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| space | 10% | 45% | 45% | 0 | 0 |
| competitive | 5% | 20% | 45% | 30% | 0 |
| selfplay | 3% | 12% | 27% | 18% | 40% |

These are proposed starting mixtures, not optimised hyperparameters. A run uses one selected stage; automatic stage progression and resuming across stages are not implemented. Historical snapshots enter after each evaluation, including the initial policy. A training side alternates each episode; opening positions and axis are varied by a separate seeded RNG. Evaluation uses paired sides, identical opening/actor/opponent seeds across comparisons, and alternates axes. The learner RNG is seeded once by the caller; the actor seed stored in training opening records is reserved for independently instantiated actors (evaluation uses it). Neural actor observations and action IDs are identical for all future algorithms; no masking and no shaping in this runner. Scripted opponents still see the full raw state as in Phase 2.

### Timing and outputs

Active time measures serial elapsed wall time for opponent sampling, game play, observation encoding and learner updates. Evaluation, file I/O and snapshot overhead are excluded from that budget and measured separately (total elapsed also includes unclassified overhead such as episode log writes and printing). Episode boundaries can overshoot the requested duration by a full episode, and evaluation checkpoints occur at the first episode boundary after an interval. One environment step is one simultaneous move, not two player moves. Run algorithms separately on the same hardware and control CPU/GPU settings; future GPU learners must synchronize asynchronous work before measuring. Episode-limited runs can be used for deterministic regression tests; time-limited runs are not guaranteed identical step counts across machines. For formal comparisons use fixed evaluation schedules, equal budgets, multiple seeds and disclose measured overshoot and setup/initialisation costs.

- `config.json`: run/algorithm/board settings and environment metadata.
- `evaluation_schedule.json`: complete fixed validation schedule.
- `episodes.jsonl`: all training games, openings, opponents, rewards, action counts and cumulative time/steps.
- `evaluations.jsonl`: checkpoint evaluations and cumulative training metrics.
- `evaluation_*.json`: raw evaluation match records.
- `checkpoints/*.npz`, `best.npz`: independent policy snapshots with metadata, selected by (wins + 0.5*draws)/games, equivalent to rescaled mean reward. They are not resume checkpoints.
- `summary.json`: total time and steps, best checkpoint, termination criterion.
- `learning_curves.png`: offline plot (created with plot command).

The included `results/phase3_smoke` is a measured 3-second reference-only run on the full board, `results/phase3_selfplay` a 1-second self-play plumbing check, and `results/phase3_holdout` an independent 16-game assessment of the first run's selected checkpoint. No actual learning improvement was established; these tiny evaluation sets simply test the framework. The original tournament results are unchanged.

## Phase 4C — Neuroevolution

This version adds `evolution`, a NumPy population-based learner. Its policy
uses the full 13-channel board and a redundant 5×5 head-centred patch, with
16 ReLU hidden units and three unmasked action scores. It requires no PyTorch
for evolution-only training. For all three algorithms and Pygame:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
pip install -e '.[dqn,ppo,play,dev,analysis]'
pytest -q
```

Train with default 16 candidates, 4 elites, 4 paired openings per candidate:

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train evolution --seconds 300 --eval-interval 60 \
  --eval-pairs 4 --stage survival --population 16 --elites 4 \
  --fitness-pairs 4 --out results/evolution_survival
python -m tron_ai.train plot results/evolution_survival
python -m tron_ai.train evaluate results/evolution_survival/best.npz \
  --pairs 10 --split holdout --out results/evolution_survival_holdout
```

The generation uses identical opponent draws and paired openings for every
candidate, ranks mean terminal reward (+1/0/-1), retains elites and mutates
random copies of those elites. Mutation defaults: 10% of parameters independently
selected, Gaussian noise standard deviation 0.08. No crossover or gradient
updates. All candidate matches count toward environment steps and active
training time, as do selection and mutation. Population initialization happens
before the runner timer, as for the other learners. Evaluation, snapshots and
logging remain separate. Checkpoints are emitted at complete-generation
boundaries, so a time budget can overshoot by a generation (potentially large
against expensive opponents). An episode cap must allow at least one full
generation and unused remainder is discarded. Generation fitness is noisy as
opponents/openings change. `episodes.jsonl` records generation and candidate for
every match; `evaluations.jsonl` includes latest best/mean/worst fitness and
population size; `evolution_diagnostics.png` visualises sampled generations.

`--init-from` loads one policy and initializes a new population from it and
mutated copies. It is NOT an exact resume of population, RNG or opponent history.
For example, to introduce stronger opponents after survival has been validated:

```bash
python -m tron_ai.train evolution --seconds 300 --stage space \
  --init-from results/evolution_survival/best.npz \
  --out results/evolution_space
```

Play with all three bundled experimental policies (keys 5, 6, 7):

```bash
python -m tron_ai.play --checkpoint results/dqn_smoke/best.npz \
  --checkpoint results/ppo_smoke/checkpoints/checkpoint_0003.npz \
  --checkpoint results/evolution_smoke/best.npz
```

These are smoke-test policies, NOT final calibrated presentation opponents.
The evolution smoke run used 12 candidates, 3 elites, 2 fitness pairs, 12 seconds,
4-second evaluation interval and 2 validation pairs. It completed 62 generations,
2,976 games and 36,931 steps in 12.186 active seconds. Fixed validation wins rose
from 4/16 to 6/16; the selected model won only 6/32 holdout games versus 9/32 for
the initial policy. This is not evidence of reliable improvement. All raw results,
plots and snapshots are included under `results/evolution_smoke`,
`results/evolution_holdout` and `results/evolution_initial_holdout`.
